# Target C: support-zero pair shadows in the final seven-no-root `k=6,p=10` branch

Date: 2026-09-21

Status: rigorous strengthening of the final support-two residual. The branch is not closed. The preceding pair-shadow reduction identifies a five-row `D=0,k=5,p=10` all-singleton boundary shadow under deletion of the two support coordinates. This note shows that at least four additional support-zero pair deletions are five-row `D=3,k=4,p=10` all-singleton shadows, and that every such `p=10` shadow has virtual-canonical deficiency at least four.

Companion checker:

- `research/verify_target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_seven_noroot_k6_dminus1_d0p10_supportzero_pair_shadows_2026_09_21.py`

The only finite inputs beyond the established deletion arithmetic are the already committed five-row `k=4` singleton-functional audits at virtual deficiency two and three.

## 1. Setup

Assume the residual hypothetical seven-row state `B` with

```text
D(B)=-1, k(B)=6, p(B)=10.
```

The preceding reduction proves that the coefficient line has support exactly

```text
S={a,b},
```

and that all ten selected incomplete root rows are singleton blocks. Deleting both `a,b` leaves all ten singleton rows present.

Let `Z` be the five zero coordinates of the coefficient line.

## 2. Every support-zero pair shadow has `D=3,k=4`

Fix one support coordinate, say `b`, and a zero coordinate `z in Z`. Put

```text
A_{b,z}=B\{b,z}.
```

The projected coefficient line is still nonzero, supported on the other support coordinate `a`, so deleting `{b,z}` causes no quotient-rank drop. The all-depth defect identity therefore gives

```text
D(A_{b,z})=-1+4=3.
```

There are five no-root rows and the projected coefficient space has rank one, hence

```text
k(A_{b,z})=4.
```

For any actual five-row state the exact root arithmetic is

```text
D=5+p+3d_root-3k.
```

Substituting `D=3,k=4` gives

```text
p+3d_root=10.
```

Thus every support-zero pair shadow has

```text
p in {10,7,4,1}.
```

Equivalently, relative to the ten global singleton rows, its root loss lies in

```text
{0,3,6,9}.
```

## 3. At least two `p=10` shadows for each support coordinate

Again fix the deleted support coordinate `b` and vary `z` over the five zero coordinates.

Every global singleton survives deletion of both support coordinates `{a,b}`. Therefore every singleton block is touched by at least one zero coordinate.

A singleton row disappears from `A_{b,z}` only when its block becomes untouched after deleting `{b,z}`. Because the block has at least one zero-coordinate touch, this can happen for at most one value of `z`: all of its zero-coordinate touches would have to be exactly that `z`, and it may additionally be touched by `b` but by no undeleted coordinate.

Hence each of the ten singleton rows contributes to the loss of at most one of the five shadows `A_{b,z}`. Therefore

```text
sum_{z in Z} (10-p(A_{b,z})) <= 10.
```

Every nonzero loss is at least three. Thus at most three of the five shadows can have `p<10`, and at least two satisfy

```text
D=3, k=4, p=10,
```

with all ten singleton rows still present.

The same argument applies after exchanging `a` and `b`. The deletion pairs on the two sides are distinct, so the seven-row residual has at least

```text
4
```

distinct five-row `D=3,k=4,p=10` all-singleton support-zero pair shadows.

## 4. Deficiency two is impossible in a `p=10` shadow

Take one of these `D=3,k=4,p=10` all-singleton shadows and apply the established rank-one virtual-canonical construction. Let `d_v` be its virtual complete-row deficiency. The five-row containment theorem gives `d_v>=2`.

If the canonical-complete singleton replacements have replacement-functional extra rank `s`, the functional accounting used in the committed `p=7` audits gives

```text
D >= 5+p+3d_v-3s.
```

At `D=3,p=10`, this requires

```text
s >= d_v+4.
```

If `d_v=2`, one would need `s>=6`. But the exact committed deficiency-two singleton-functional audit proves that whenever enough compatible singleton blocks exist, the span of all such singleton functionals has extra rank exactly two. Thus `d_v=2` is impossible.

## 5. Deficiency three is impossible

If `d_v=3`, the same defect equation requires

```text
s>=7.
```

This already forces at least seven compatible canonical-complete singleton blocks, exactly the class covered by the committed deficiency-three functional audit. That exhaustive audit proves that the span of all compatible singleton functionals has extra rank at most six.

Therefore `d_v=3` is also impossible.

Hence every `D=3,k=4,p=10` all-singleton pair shadow forced above satisfies

```text
d_v>=4.
```

## 6. Only three virtual deficiency/rank thresholds remain

There are only ten singleton incomplete blocks, so at most ten canonical-complete singleton replacement functionals are available. Therefore

```text
s<=10.
```

Combining this with `s>=d_v+4` gives

```text
d_v<=6.
```

Every forced `p=10` support-zero pair shadow is therefore in exactly one of the three virtual ranges

```text
d_v=4: required singleton-functional extra rank >=8,
d_v=5: required singleton-functional extra rank >=9,
d_v=6: required singleton-functional extra rank =10.
```

In particular it must have at least 8, 9, or 10 canonical-complete singleton-compatible blocks respectively.

So the final seven-row `k=6` residual simultaneously forces:

1. one five-row `D=0,k=5,p=10` all-singleton shadow under deletion of the support pair, whose two deleted cycles must form the nonzero projective quotient-residual collision described in the preceding note; and
2. at least four five-row `D=3,k=4,p=10` all-singleton shadows with virtual deficiency in `{4,5,6}` and required singleton-functional extra ranks `{8,9,10}`.

This gives a second exact finite attack. A five-row `k=4,p=10` functional audit only needs to inspect the three classes

```text
(d_v, singleton capacity lower bound) = (4,8), (5,9), (6,10).
```

If none reaches functional extra rank `d_v+4`, the entire seven-no-root `k=6` branch closes without classifying the `k=5,p=10` sharp boundary.

